WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.162
Why can't you defend what you can't see?

2
00:00:02.162 --> 00:00:05.007
Most agencies run a SIEM,
pay for log storage,

3
00:00:05.007 --> 00:00:08.176
and can show an auditor
millions of events a day.

4
00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:10.200
But each of those measures what arrived.

5
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.800
None of them measures what didn't.

6
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.137
NIST keeps apart two things
that are easy to buy as one.

7
00:00:15.137 --> 00:00:18.400
AU-12 says generate the audit records.

8
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.826
AU-6 says review and analyze
them for unusual activity

9
00:00:22.826 --> 00:00:27.350
and report what you find, and
SI-4 adds detecting attacks.

10
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.825
An agency can satisfy the first
completely and still fail the other two.

11
00:00:31.825 --> 00:00:36.425
OMB M-26-14 measures the same way.

12
00:00:36.425 --> 00:00:40.596
Logs must be searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year,

13
00:00:40.596 --> 00:00:45.154
but its maturity model scores collection
by alerting: under half of the

14
00:00:45.154 --> 00:00:50.175
baseline covered by actionable alerts at
Level 1, at least 70 percent at Level 3.

15
00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:53.303
And Microsoft says logs
ingested only into the

16
00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:56.350
Sentinel data lake tier
don't generate alerts.

17
00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.210
That source meets retention
and detects nothing.
